    "trigger_card_number": 176,
    "title": "LESSON 16: THE TER- FORMS",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "   Apa yang terjadi di sekolah kemarin?",
          "   = What happened at school yesterday?",
          "",
          "ter- is the prefix of things that simply",
          "are, or that came about without anyone",
          "meaning them to. Nobody did it.",
          "",
          "   jadi    become  terjadi    happened",
          "   lihat   see     terlihat   is visible",
          "   dengar  hear    terdengar  is audible",
          "   buka    open    terbuka    is open",
          "   tutup   shut    tertutup   is shut",
          "",
          "   Pintu kamar itu terbuka sejak pagi.",
          "   = The bedroom door has been open since",
          "     morning.",
          "",
          "Nothing in that sentence opened it."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Where di- says somebody did it, ter- says",
          "it is simply so:",
          "",
          "   dibuka   was opened, by somebody",
          "   terbuka  is open, as a state",
          "",
          "With people, ter- often means it happened",
          "to them, and they did not choose it:",
          "",
          "   terluka    injured",
          "   terkejut   taken aback",
          "   tertarik   interested, drawn to",
          "   terlambat  late",
          "   Anak itu terluka di kaki.",
          "   = The child was hurt on the leg.",
          "",
          "ternyata is the same prefix on nyata,",
          "real. It means: as it turns out."
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
